.TH SYS_IDENT 2
.SH NAME
sys_ident \- who a process is (SYS_IDENT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_ident(int pid, mk_ident_t *id);

typedef struct { int32_t n, g[NGROUPS]; } mk_groups_t;
typedef struct {
    int32_t pid, uid, gid;
    mk_groups_t groups;   /* the other groups, at most 16 */
    char console[16];     /* its terminal */
} mk_ident_t;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_ident
fills
.I id
with the identity of process
.IR pid ,
or of the caller if
.I pid
is 0: its pid, user, primary group, the other groups it belongs to, and
its terminal ("port" or "port#channel"; empty: the system console, port
"console").
.PP
The kernel stamps uid and gid on every message; a server asks for the
other groups with this call only when they matter. A process's identity
never changes, so the answer may be kept. The terminal is inherited by
children: the client library sends its console requests there, which is
how a shell in a window of the desktop talks to that window.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
No such process (or it has ended).
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I id
is not writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR id (1),
.BR security (7)
